RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
 Office of City Clerk
April 25, 2016
The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne met in regular session on this date beginning
at 6:00 p.m. in Council Chambers.  Present were: MAYOR - Richard L. Kaysen; COUNCIL
MEMBERS - Jim Brown, Bryan Cook, Richard Johnson, Mike Luna, Dr. Mark Rinne, Scott Roybal,
Dicky Shanor, Annette Williams and Jeff White.  Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk, and Dan
White, City Attorney.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.
Consent Agenda.  (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to be
routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion. There will be no separate
discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and support by two
other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be considered in its
normal sequence on the agenda.  Upon request by Dr. Rinne, and pursuant to support received by
Mr. Shanor and Mr. Roybal, item #20(a) was removed from the Consent Agenda.  Mr. Shanor
moved to approve remaining Consent Agenda items, seconded by Mr. Cook.  Motion carried.
Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body.
[CA]
Minutes from Regular Meeting of the Governing Body on April 11, 2016.  Motion (per
Consent Agenda) carried.
Mr. Brown declared a conflict of interest (business interest in voucher payable to Ameri-Tech
HVAC Services) and vacated Council Chambers.  Mr. Luna moved to approve payment of vouchers
as presented, seconded by Mr. Shanor.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the
governing body present in Council Chambers.  Following announcement of the vote, Mr. Brown
returned to Council Chambers.
Mayor Kaysen opened a Public Hearing to receive comments and input for the City of
Cheyenne Annual Action Plan 2016 - 2017, City of Cheyenne Community Development Block Grant
(CDBG) entitlement.  Deanne Widauf, Housing and Community Development Office, provided a
brief overview of the history and purpose of the CDBG program, noting the City has received
entitlement grant funds since 1975.  She advised the proposed plan is consistent with the 5 year
Comprehensive Consolidated Plan completed in 2015, and stated the anticipated allocation funding
amount is $397,441.00 with anticipated program income of $1,000.00 and $135,578.00 in recaptured
funding.  Ms Widauf explained the grant application process, public notifications and opportunities
for input, and role of the Advisory Council in reviewing and recommending projects to be funded.
She stated 13 projects are proposed under the categories of public services and general services, and
noted funding for program administration.  She reviewed the projects and funding amounts as
recommended by the Advisory Council.  There being no further comments and input, and purpose
of the hearing having been met, Mayor Kaysen closed the hearing.
Mayor Kaysen opened a Public Hearing to receive comments and input for a transfer of
```

### Page 2 of 8

```text
ownership for a restaurant liquor license filed with the City Clerk’s Office for Twin Dragon Chinese
Food LLC, d/b/a Twin Dragon, 1809 Carey Avenue, Cheyenne, Wyoming (License currently held
by Twin Dragon Chinese Restaurant, Inc. d/b/a Twin Dragon, 1809 Carey Avenue, Cheyenne,
Wyoming).  Don Christianson, representative for applicant,  advised he was available to respond to
any questions.  There being no comments or input, Mayor Kaysen closed the hearing and referred
the application to the Finance Committee.
Mayor Kaysen opened a Public Hearing to receive comments and input on the “2016
Downtown Cheyenne Core Plan” as proposed by the Downtown Development Authority of the City
of Cheyenne.  Darren Rudloff, Visit Cheyenne, and Amy Surdam, Cheyenne Downtown
Development Authority, provided information on the development of the Core Plan.  They advised
proposed projects affiliated with the plan are focused within a six block area around the Depot Plaza,
and referred to as the downtown “core”; that the action plan provides for a short list of projects at
relatively low cost, and noted financial assistance for some of the projects will be available.
Appreciation was expressed for the economic development partners that were involved in developing
the plan.  There being no further comments or input, Mayor Kaysen closed the hearing.
Mayor Kaysen opened a Public Hearing to receive comments and input regarding the intent
of the City of Cheyenne to submit an application to the Wyoming Business Council’s Business
Ready Community, Downtown Development Grant Program, to assist in funding the Downtown
SkyPath Parking Garage Connector Project.  Cindy Schneider, Grants Manager, explained the intent
of the grant and use of the funds, if received, for a raised walkway from the Jack R. Spiker Parking
Structure to Capitol Avenue (Carey Avenue and through alley) to connect with the Hynds Building
to promote economic development.  The walkway will be a public facility and will have connections
available to adjacent businesses at the business owner’s expense.  She advised a developer of the
Hynds Building has a tenant interested in occupying the building and the application is in
conjunction with their request for the SkyPath to provide parking access, and noted the project will
have a positive effect on the development of the Hynds Building, which has been largely vacant for
30 years, and the downtown.  She stated this is a cooperative project between the DDA, Cheyenne
LEADS, Chamber of Commerce, Visit Cheyenne and the Hynds Building project developer and the
potential tenant.  Ms. Schneider stated the project is estimated to cost $2.5 million dollars and that
the maximum grant request under the program is $2.0 million dollars.  She further advised the
application is due May 15, 2016 and will be considered by the Wyoming Business Council in
September.  Anja Bendel, Director of Development, Cheyenne LEADS, advised that representatives
from all four economic development organizations were present and in support of the project. She
explained the proposed SkyPath is incorporated into the Downtown Core Plan and stated the project
is important for redevelopment of the Hynds Building, the alley way behind it and all properties
along the block by providing parking access.  Upon inquiry as to the source of funding for the City’s
grant match ($450,000.00), Mayor Kaysen explained determination of where the funding match
would come from would be by the governing body.  There being no further comments or input,
Mayor Kaysen closed the public hearing.
Public Hearing to receive public comment regarding the intent of the City of Cheyenne to
submit an application to the Wyoming Business Council’s Business Ready Community, Downtown
Development Grant Program,  to assist in funding the Hynds Building Facade Improvement Project.
Mayor Kaysen advised the application for this project was being postponed, therefore, there was no
