```text
longer a purpose to conduct the public hearing and announced the hearing was cancelled.
ORDINANCE - 3
 READING - Repealing Section 9.16.030, Minors on Premises of
RD
Billiard or Pool Hall, of Chapter 9.16, Offenses by or Against Minors, of Title 9, Public Peace and
Welfare, of the Code of the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming.  Reporting for Public Services Committee,
Dr. Rinne moved to approve on third and final reading, seconded by Ms. Williams.  Motion carried.
Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body. (#4125)
ORDINANCE - 3
 READING - Amending Section 6.08.020, Keeping or Slaughtering of
RD
Certain Animals - Restrictions Within the City, of Chapter 6.08, Animal Care and Control
Regulations Generally, of Title 6, Animals, of the Code of the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming.  Julie
Gliem, Cheyenne Landlords Association, commented on concerns with the ordinance relative to
landlords and tenants and restrictive use of property; scenarios involving special service animals;
abandoned or deceased chickens and responsible parties, and advised that landlords in general aren’t
in support of the ordinance.  Speaking in support of the ordinance generally were: Victoria Fry,
Rebecca Pike, Landon Brown, Lisa Glauner, Lucy Pauley, Jessica Fehlberg and Lindsay Simineo.
Topics discussed were: Cities that currently allow backyard chickens; future development and
promoting a progressive image of Cheyenne; benefits of chickens and value of locally raised food;
concern with acquiring permission from adjacent property owners; public support for the ordinance
through local media polls, and therapeutic animals.  Major Dorr spoke in opposition to the ordinance,
commenting on ordinance requirements and concerns involving adjacent property owner
permissions, procedures and records, and the ordinance potentially creating a neighborhood
“friendship” problem.  During her comments, Ms. Pauley stated she was prepared to donate a coop
to the Cheyenne Animal Shelter, if needed, relative to concerns expressed with abandoned chickens
being housed at the Shelter.  Catherine Wissner, UW Laramie County Extension Office, reiterated
her offer made at the April 11 City Council meeting regarding community education classes on
th
chicken care and owner responsibility.  Upon inquiry from Wesley Frain, Dan White, City Attorney,
advised the ordinance will require permission from adjacent land owners and applicable tenants.
Reporting for Committee of the Whole, Mr. Cook moved to approve on third and final reading,
seconded by Mr. Roybal.  Upon inquiry, Ms. Gliem explained that a lot of Cheyenne property
owners do not reside locally; that  land owners may be different than structural property owners, and,
in some cases, owners may be deceased.  Mr. Cook moved to amend by substitute ordinance,
seconded by Mr. Shanor.  Mr. Shanor and Mr. Roybal provided information on the changes
contained in the proposed substitute.  Boyd Wiggam, Wyoming Liberty Group, spoke in support of
the proposed amendment. Comment was made on the change of increasing the number of allowable
hens to six and future consideration of amending a section of City Code involving quantities of
animals that can be purchased.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” -- Mr. Brown, Mr. Johnson,
Mr. Luna, Mr. Roybal, Mr. Shanor and Mr. White.  Voting “no” -- Mr. Cook, Dr. Rinne, Ms.
Williams and Mayor Kaysen.  Discussion on the main motion as amended followed and included
the following topics: Property tenant and owner approvals; covenants vs. ordinances; neighborhood
conflicts; responsibility of chicken owners; progressive communities; communities that allow urban
chickens; ordinance effect on Cheyenne Animal Shelter; investment of owning chickens; training
offered and potential neighborhood conflicts.  Upon inquiry during discussion, Ms. Gliem provided
information on landlord issues, and Robert Fecht, Cheyenne Animal Shelter, stated his belief that
the ordinance will result in an impact of some kind to the Shelter but what that impact may be is
```

